210:{character:Auctioneer}9 million Coppers going twice.
211:{character:Auctioneer}9 million Coppers going three times.
212:{character:Auctioneer}The coral red skimmia has been sold at the price of 9 million Coppers. Congratulations to you, Buyer No.6.
213:The crisp sound of the mallet announces the sale. Now I only have one option left, the snow tower peony. No matter what, I must get it for 10 million Coppers or less, or else I will have achieved nothing today.
214:{character:Noah}(Text message) The snow peonies in this flower arrangement are grown as a pair, and it's quite rare for them to grow to this size. According to Strix's information database, this is Countless Springs's latest flower breed, and this is the first time it is being sold in their auction.
215:{character:player}(Text message) Doesn't that mean there's no price information to refer to?
216:{character:Noah}(Text message) Yes, there isn't any, so do your best.
217:When I think about how this is the first time they are selling this flower arrangement in the auction and how Kurone Toujou is participating in this time's auction, I get a strong feeling that this is the one I need to get, but... when I touch the card in my pocket with the balance of only 10 million Coppers, I hope that the competition won't be too fierce.
218:The auction for the last flower arrangement starts after a short break. I sit on the edge the whole time while the auctioneer calls out the price, but fortunately, nobody raises their number plate. When the auctioneer calls out 10 million Coppers...
219:This is the last flower arrangement. I'll definitely fail this task if I don't get it. With this thought in mind, I decisively raise my number plate. The auctioneer looks at me and starts calling out the bid after nodding at me.
220:{character:Auctioneer}The snow tower peony has been sold at the price of 10 million Coppers. Congratulations to you, Buyer No.17.
221:{character:Noah}(Text message) I can't say for sure.
222:"Think before you act." This is one of my mottos that I've been following in One Han City. Instead of raising my number plate rashly, I should observe everyone else instead.
223:The venue is obviously quieter than before. It looks like the appearance of this new cut flower has made everyone uncertain about their course of action and the price range of this flower arrangement. In that case, I can wait and see if I want to raise my number plate next time.
224:The auctioneer calls out the next price. Since she just called 10 million Coppers, the next price is 9 million Coppers.
225:I wait silently for a while and notice that nobody has raised their number plate yet, which means I have a high chance of winning the bid for this flower arrangement if I raise my number plate now, but one of my other mottos is "keep waiting and you might be in for a surprise."
226:What should I do?
227:The main reason I'm participating in this auction is to complete a task, so it's better not to take too many risks. Although there's a chance to get it for a cheaper price, this risk increases as the price decreases, so I might as well get it now.
228:Not to mention, I'll be spending Kutsuji's money. When I remember how overbearing he was, I feel like my hand will relax and I'll become happier when I swipe the card and enter the password later.
229:With this thought in mind, I decisively raise my number plate. However, another buyer sitting in front of me on the left also raises their number plate at the same time. According to the rules of the auction, we will now make increasing bids starting from 9 million Coppers, and each bid must be at least an increase of half a million Coppers.
230:{character:Auctioneer}Buyer No.17 raised their number plate first, so according to the rules, Buyer No.22 will call their bid.
231:Buyer No.22
232:{character:Buyer No.22}9.5 million Coppers.
233:I quickly call out the price of 10 million Coppers in response. Buyer No.22 looks back at me and falls deep into thought.
234:After the waiting time ends, the auctioneer looks at me and starts calling out the bid routinely after nodding at me.
235:The crisp sound of the mallet announces my success, but before I can share my joy with Noah, I see her frowning and muttering something under her breath.
236:Since nobody has taken action yet, I decide to keep waiting, not because I want to help Kutsuji save some money, but mainly because I enjoy this exciting feeling.
237:But since I saw some doubt in Noah's face, I decide to send her a text message.
238:{character:player}(Text message) I want to help Strix save some money. I hope Kutsuji will treat me nicer in the future since I've got his best interests in mind.
239:{character:Noah}(Text message) I've passed on your message.
240:The auctioneer calls out the price of 8 million Coppers at the stand...
241:Considering the transaction price of the first two flower arrangements, I decide it's time to raise my number plate. Unexpectedly, another person has the same idea as me. According to the rules of the auction, we will now make increasing bids starting from 8 million Coppers, and each bid must be at least an increase of half a million Coppers.
242:{character:Auctioneer}Buyer No.12 raised their number plate first, so according to the rules, Buyer No.17 will call their bid.
243:{character:player}8.5 million.
244:Buyer No.12
245:{character:Buyer No.12}I pass.
246:The other buyer resolutely gives up after I call out the price of 8.5 million Coppers. The auctioneer looks at me and starts calling out the bid routinely after nodding at me.
247:{character:Auctioneer}8.5 million Coppers going once.
248:{character:Auctioneer}8.5 million Coppers going twice.
249:{character:Auctioneer}8.5 million Coppers going three times.
250:{character:Auctioneer}The snow tower peony has been sold at the price of 8.5 million Coppers. Congratulations to you, Buyer No.17.
251:The crisp sound of the mallet announces my success, but before I can share my joy with Noah, I see her frowning and muttering something under her breath.
252:How should I say this? The whole point of this is the excitement. As long as you're brave enough... Just as I'm about to continue challenging the limits, the person sitting next to me raises their number plate. After freezing for a moment, I quickly raise my own number plate.
253:According to the rules of the auction, we will now make increasing bids starting from 8 million Coppers, and each bid must be at least an increase of half a million Coppers.
254:The crisp sound of the mallet announces my success. I let out a long breath. This really is very exciting!
255:Just as I'm about to share my joy with Noah, I see her frowning and muttering something under her breath.
256:After thinking about it for a while, I decide to bid on the second flower arrangement, the coral red skimmia. Since I've made up my mind, the first auction no longer concerns me. I sit quietly and watch the auction with the intention to analyze the information I have.
257:{character:Noah}(Text message) Why aren't you considering placing a bid from the first flower arrangement?
258:{character:player}(Text message) If I can only win the bid for one flower arrangement, that means I have a one-in-three chance of getting the right one. There's no guarantee that the first one is the one we need.
259:{character:Noah}(Text message) I'm surprised that you understand probability.
260:{character:player}(Text message) Shouldn't anyone who has received a compulsory education know something like this, which is like basic common sense...?
261:{character:player}(Text message) Does everyone in Strix like to attack everyone else verbally?
262:{character:Noah}(Text message) No, that's just the boss's personal hobby.
263:{character:Noah}(Text message) Actually, he was already quite nice to you last time. If you want to see what the boss is usually like when he mocks people, you just have to fail this task.
264:{character:player}(Text message) That's not necessary. Seriously.
265:{character:Noah}(Text message) But after interacting with you, I'm starting to understand why the boss is interested in you.
266:{character:player}(Text message) ...You're welcome.
266:{character:Me}(Text message) ...You're welcome.
267:While I chat with Noah, the first flower arrangement quickly sells for the price of 7.5 million Coppers.
268:{character:Noah}(Text message) The flowing gold camellia sold for 6.5 million Coppers last year. The price increased by one million this year. Maybe you can use this as a reference when you place a bid later.
269:{character:Noah}(Text message) The coral red skimmia that will be auctioned later was sold for 6.3 million Coppers and 7.1 million Coppers in the last two years.
270:{character:Noah}(Text message) But according to the information I just received, this year's flower arrangement looks superior to the ones sold in the last two years.
271:{character:player}(Text message) K.
272:After replying with a simple "K," I begin to listen carefully to the prices called out by the auctioneer.
273:The crisp sound of the mallet announces my success since nobody is competing with me, but before I can share my joy with Noah, I hear a sigh next to me.
274:The auctioneer continues to lower the price. To my surprise, someone raises their number plate when the price reaches 9 million Coppers this time. I wonder if the people here have been stimulated by the higher transaction price of the flowing gold camellia just now. It could also be because the flower looks better than the one last year like Noah mentioned, and some regulars might think this is a signal.
275:Kutsuji has only given me 10 million Coppers to use. Rather than failing to get the next flower arrangement, I rather take a gamble with this one, so I raise my number plate.
276:The crisp sound of the mallet announces my success. The other buyer chooses to give up, probably because they don't think it's worth 10 million Coppers. That's true though. If they wanted to buy it for 10 million Coppers, they could have raised their number plate earlier.
277:"Think before you act." This is one of my mottos that I've been following in One Han City. Instead of raising my number plate rashly, I should observe everyone else instead.
278:The venue is obviously quieter than before. It looks like the appearance of this new cut flower has made everyone uncertain about their course of action and the price range of this flower arrangement. In that case, I can wait and see if I want to raise my number plate next time.
279:According to the rules of the auction, we will now make increasing bids starting from 8 million Coppers, and each bid must be at least an increase of half a million Coppers.
280:After thinking about it for a while, I decide to bid on the third flower arrangement, the snow tower peony. Since I've made up my mind, the first two auctions no longer concern me. I sit quietly and watch the auction with the intention to analyze the information I have.
281:While I chat with Noah, the first two flower arrangements quickly sell for the prices of 7.5 million Coppers and 9 million Coppers.
285:The crisp sound of the mallet announces my success, but before I can share my joy with Noah, I hear a sigh next to me.
286:I wait silently for a while and notice that nobody has raised their number plate yet, which means I have a high chance of winning the bid for this flower arrangement if I raise my number plate now, but one of my other mottos is "keep waiting and you might be in for a surprise."
288:{character:Noah}(Text message) I think you should deal with the immediate crisis first.
290:{character:Noah}(Text message) A background check has been completed in advance on all the buyers who are eligible for the auction.
291:{character:player}(Text message) Seriously? Is it impossible to have a little privacy on the Internet?
292:{character:Noah}(Text message) Based on what I know, judging from how frequently you receive sales calls from various agencies recently, you don't have much privacy in real life either.
293:{character:player}(Text message) Can't you be nice and avoid mentioning things that hurt?
294:{character:Noah}(Text message) Let's get back to the matter at hand. (I wonder why it's so easy to start talking about other things when I'm talking to you. I hope you'll reflect on this.)
295:{character:player}(Text message) Okay, continue. (You can't clap with one hand. We should both reflect on this.)
296:{character:Noah}(Text message) I'm pretty sure that they investigated your living habits when they did the background check. How you're spending a fortune today for an afternoon tea isn't at all consistent with everyone's perception of you.
297:{character:Noah}(Text message) For example, when you pull for characters in a game, you wouldn't spend a lot of money on a character that can only be used for two hours, right?
300:{character:Narration}I wait for a long time for the person who notified me to complete the next part of the transaction, but no one shows up. When I look up, I happen to see two staff members behind a nearby pillar looking in our direction, who seem to be discussing something.
301:{character:player}(Text message) What should I do now?
302:{character:Noah}(Text message) Countless Springs is a legal business. In the worst-case scenario, they will take away your right to complete the transaction. They won't harm you.
303:{character:player}(Text message) That's good news.
304:{character:Noah}(Text message) At most, the boss will just exact revenge on you.
305:{character:player}(Text message) ...May there be no Kutsuji in heaven.
306:The two staff members seem to have finished their discussion and start to walk towards us. I close the text messaging screen of my conversation with Noah and act as calm, composed, and relaxed as possible.
307:Staff A
308:{character:Staff A}Buyer No.17, please follow us to the back to complete the next part of the transaction.
309:Staff B
310:{character:Staff B}The lady with you will need to wait here.
311:{character:player}Can't we go together?
312:{character:Staff A}Sorry, according to our rules, you, the buyer, must complete the next part of the transaction alone.
313:Noah pats my shoulder and nods at me. According to my understanding, she is telling me to relax and go, but this situation is like trying to complete a task alone in an escape room game. Even if all your team members are cheering for you and saying things like "good luck" or "you can do it" to you, it doesn't mean you won't worry about those NPCs who are hiding and can rush out to scare you at any time.
314:I follow the staff through a corridor that isn't too long and see something familiar in the middle of a room--a mahjong table, which surprises me. Will I be paying in the mahjong room?
315:{character:Staff A}According to the rules, you'll need to play a game of three-player mahjong with us first.
316:{character:player}Although I know this is One Han City, isn't this rule a little too formulaic? It almost sounds like it's necessary to verify everything with a game of mahjong before doing anything in One Han City.
317:{character:Staff B}You don't need to worry. It's just one game of three-player mahjong, so it won't take long. However, you'll need to wear a device.
318:{character:player}A device?
319:I watch as they walk over with some metal patches connected to a huge instrument behind them and quickly attach them to my carotid artery, temples, etc., without giving me a chance to say no.
320:I can feel micro-currents from the thing they attached to me and play a game of three-player mahjong at the same time. Although I sit down at the mahjong table and prepare to play a game as they requested, there's a huge question mark in my head.
321:As it's necessary to adjust the mode of automatic mahjong machines to play three-player mahjong in One Han City, most dojos find this troublesome and refuse to let players play three-player mahjong. However, according to a manager of a dojo who is unwilling to disclose her age and name, the main reason is that dojos make less money per table when people play three-player mahjong than four-player mahjong, which makes sense as there is one less person paying per table.
322:For this reason, I don't have much experience with playing three-player mahjong in person. But I can only bite the bullet now that things have come to this.
323:{character:player}Hmm... I almost knocked a tile over. It's so inconvenient playing mahjong while wearing these things...
324:A wire hanging around my wrist makes me almost knock over a tile at the beginning of the game. However, the two staff members facing me just smile reassuringly at me with a look of understanding on their faces without expressing any intention to take off the things restraining me. Since there's nothing I can do about it, I try to keep my movements small and controlled to lower the chance of making a mistake in a physical sense.
325:Three-player mahjong is special not just because it has special rules such as reduced tiles and the Kita rule. Compared with four-player mahjong, it is more of a winner-take-all game, so the two are quite different in terms of making offense and defense related decisions.
326:As only a few Yaku need 1 Man and 9 Man tiles, these two Terminals tiles are less important than usual. I hope I won't see many of these two tiles in my hand.
327:Worrying about something really does seem to make it more likely to happen. Fortunately, I drew a pair of 1 Man that can be used as a Janto. Based on my current hand, although my Pin tiles form a Closed Wait, my Sou tiles have already formed a Mentsu.
328:In addition, as I have 1 West Wind in my hand which is my seat wind, and having 1 North Wind also guarantees that I can score 1 Han of points, my hand is decent. If I can get another West Wind tile to form a pair, I will be able to finish the game quickly.
329:{character:Staff A}West.
330:{character:Staff B}Same.
331:{character:player}H-Haha...
332:Like a game of mahjong, life has its ups and downs. My wishful thinking didn't work out. Sighing secretly in my heart, I can only hope the first tile I draw is good.
333:However, I draw a 9 Man, which means I started this game with basically two useless tiles. What a depressing start.
334:Should I play Kita and wait for another chance?
335:{character:player}Kita.
336:After taking a deep breath, I reach for the next tile. I'm so happy I drew a 6 Pin.
337:After playing Kita, I draw a 6 Pin, which gives me a good hand. My hand is starting to look good in this game.
338:I consider my options and then discard my West Wind for tile efficiency. Although the 9 Man will only be useful if I draw a Triplet, I have a high chance of catching a deal-in if I get a dual pair of 1 Man and 9 Man tiles in the Riichi phase.
339:As for the risks I face, there's a chance that my opponents might put together Thirteen Orphans if I play it a few turns later, but since the two other players have already discarded the West Wind tile, it doesn't seem likely that they would already have Thirteen Orphans in their starting hand as well as an extra West Wind... right?
340:{character:player}Um... West Wind.
341:I discard my West Wind for tile efficiency. Although the 9 Man will only be useful if I draw a Triplet, I have a high chance of catching a deal-in if I get a dual pair of 1 Man and 9 Man tiles in the Riichi phase.
342:As for the risks I face, there's a chance that my opponents might put together Thirteen Orphans if I play it a few turns later, but the two other players have already discarded a West Wind tile. They can't possibly have redundant tiles for Thirteen Orphans in their starting hand... right?
343:As for my North Wind, sometimes it's a useful safe tile to play when another player is in the Riichi phase, so I decide to keep it for now.
344:{character:Staff B}Riichi.
345:Upon hearing the player in front of me call Riichi, I subconsciously look at his discarded tiles...
346:Except for the 9 Man and South Wind, every other tile was discarded from the hand...
347:My hand has been in one away from Tenpai state since two turns ago. Drawing a 6 Sou in this turn has made my hand better, but now I also have too many potential Mentsu.
348:Since the player before me discarded a 2 Sou and 7 Sou from their hand consecutively, it would be very risky for me to discard the 3 Sou in my hand. So I can't break up my 1, 3 Sou combination. If I want to go on the offensive, I'll need to break up another combination, but...
349:First a 4 and then a 2... Judging from the seat order, it looks like the player before me has broken up a badly formed wait, but I can't rule out the possibility that they have 1, 1, 2, 4 Sou in their hand and are waiting for a 1 Pin for a dual pair.
350:This is my first time playing with the player before me, so I'm not sure about their playing habits. What if they have 2, 4, 4, 5 Pin in their hand and are choosing to play good tiles to form a better hand?
351:{character:Staff A}There's a change in the heart rate. Record it.
352:{character:Staff B}Okay.
353:{character:player}That's not necessary...
354:I need to calm down... In the end, I overlooked one thing. Whether I should start attacking should be determined by the purpose of this test and not how safe my hand is. In this situation, what would the organizer want me to do?
355:Arguably, luck is a major factor that determines the outcome of a one-game mahjong match, and one can't fully demonstrate their abilities even if they win such a game.
356:But... luck plays a major part in today's auction of the cut flowers. Is it possible that this is what they want to see?
357:The outcome of this game might affect the next part of the transaction. When I think of this, I can't make myself give up just like this.
358:What should I do?
359:After thinking about it for a long time, I decide to ignore the possibility of a dual pair. I discard a 1 Pin to see what will happen.
360:If this game is meant to test my determination, then backing down here could be seen as playing conservatively and not taking any risks, which could make them think that I'm not that determined to meet Kurone Toujou.
361:Luckily, nothing happens after I discard the 1 Pin. In the next turn, the player before me plays a 3 Pin and I manage to draw a 2 Sou and Tenpai in the same turn. To increase my chances of winning the game, I choose to Damaten.
362:{character:player}...I'll take that 7 Sou. I win.
363:In the end, I manage to get a 7 Sou from the player opposite me. The two staff members get out of their seats and help me remove the testing instrument.
364:Now that I think about it, the staff members didn't say that I had to win the game. Is it possible that this is actually a test of my prudence? After all, Kurone Toujou is an educated and well-mannered geisha, so she probably doesn't want to receive any reckless guests.
365:Based on my above consideration, I choose the most prudent option. I discard the 7 Sou the other player just discarded.
366:It's such a pity that I drew a 2 Sou in the next turn and the opponent opposite me also discarded a 7 Sou. If I broke up my 1, 3 Pin combination and kept attacking, I would win the match now.
367:However... It's a little early to conclude that my previous decision was wrong. I put my regrets behind me and continue to defend by discarding other safe tiles.
368:{character:Staff A}Thanks for your hard work.
369:This game ends in a draw. The two staff members get out of their seats and help me remove the testing instrument.
370:{character:Staff A}Sorry about making you so worried. Actually, we just wanted to check whether you are who you claim to be through a mahjong game.
371:{character:Staff B}According to our background check, you aren't a person who spends money without thinking things through, so we added this test to the process.
372:{character:player}You can find out whether I am who I claim to be by playing a game of three-player mahjong?
373:The last time something like this happened was when my biology teacher in middle school wanted us to identify insects by their legs, and even that sounds more scientific than what they are doing now!
374:{character:Staff A}This instrument monitors the changes in your vital signs during the game and compares them with the data of when you usually play mahjong to confirm whether they match your mahjong style.
375:I'm shocked and unable to comprehend what they are telling me, but I indicate my acceptance.
376:They seem to receive an order in their earpiece at this moment. They turn sideways and have a short exchange with the person speaking to them, then they turn around to face me and start clapping.
377:{character:Staff A}Congratulations on passing our test. You have won the chance to attend an exclusive afternoon tea session at Countless Springs tomorrow, and you'll be accompanied by Miss Toujou at that time.
378:{character:player}Miss Toujou... Is that Kurone Toujou?
379:{character:Staff A}Yes, you're really lucky.
380:Everything still feels unreal until I swipe the card and complete the transaction. I actually bid on the right cut flower and won the chance to have afternoon tea with Kurone Toujou. Is it possible that Kutsuji got me to do this because he wanted to take advantage of my luck?
381:If I knew I would be so lucky today, I would have bought a lottery ticket!
382:When I carefully carry the cut flower arrangement to the entrance of Countless Springs, I see that Noah is already waiting for me there. The moment I see her, I feel like I can't wait to share my joy and the unbelievable experience I just had with her...
383:{character:player}Do you know what I just experienced?
384:{character:player}They monitored my vital signs with an instrument while I played a game of three-player mahjong to determine if I am who I claimed to be. It's outrageous!!!
385:{character:Noah}(Text message) In games, when the plot doesn't make sense, it's very common to write something magical to retcon it.
386:{character:Noah}(Text message) You should play more games. You'll laugh at the story, and then you'll start to understand it, make it a part of you, and then surpass it.
387:{character:player}But this is the real world!
388:{character:Noah}(Text message) The real world is usually more magical than the world in games. How else can art come from life, and how can fan artwork surpass the original artwork?
389:{character:Noah}(Text message) There are many unbelievable things like this in One Han City. If you don't want to panic the next time you encounter something like this, you can buy the relevant information from Strix first.
390:{character:player}What you said makes sense... if you didn't add that advertisement at the end. I almost believed you.
391:{character:player}Oh right. This is the cut flowers we bought. This is the one that represents Kurone Toujou.
392:I hand the cut flowers in my hands to Noah, but she shakes her head and hands me one of her earphones instead.
393:A voice message starts playing, and I hear an arrogant voice speaking.
395:{character:Kutsuji}(Voice message) Noah, you don't need to bring the flowers back with you later. Give them to {var:player_nickname} as compensation for the fright. But remember to remind {var:player_nickname} that any extra money spent will have to be compensated with labor.
396:Noah pats my shoulder and pushes the flowers into my arms, then she turns and gets into a red car next to her.
397:The car disappears quickly from my sight like a bolt of lightning... Geez, can't you give me a ride home at least? You don't need to burn the bridge after crossing the river.
398:I start walking towards the subway station. On my way there, my mobile phone starts vibrating again, and I just know it's Noah without looking at the sender.
399:{character:Noah}(Text message) Have a good rest. I'll come to pick you up tomorrow.
400:I rub my belly. What's done is done, so I decide to find a place to eat first. At least the task went well. I'll worry about tomorrow's affairs tomorrow.
401:{character:Noah}(Text message) There's something you did really well.
408:{character:Noah}(Text message) I'm pretty sure that they investigated your living habits when they did the background check. Today, you acted just like how you usually act, so you won't make them suspicious of you.
409:Immediately after I read the message Noah sent me, I see two staff members walking towards us.
410:{character:Staff A}Please follow us to the back to complete the next part of the transaction.
411:Noah and I exchange glances, then we get up and follow them through a long corridor to a beautifully decorated reception room. The cut flower arrangement I won at the auction is sitting there on a coffee table.
412:Everything goes well with Noah accompanying me. After completing the transaction, the staff member congratulates me.
413:{character:Staff A}Congratulations! You have won the chance to attend an exclusive afternoon tea session at Countless Springs tomorrow, and you'll be accompanied by Miss Toujou at that time.
414:I actually bid on the right cut flower and won the chance to have afternoon tea with Kurone Toujou. Is it possible that Kutsuji got me to do this because he wanted to take advantage of my luck?
415:I do my best not to look too excited in case they start suspecting that I have a specific purpose in mind. I hold the flowers carefully when I leave the room with Noah. When we arrive at the entrance of Countless Springs, Noah pats my shoulder and sends me a text message.
416:{character:Noah}(Text message) You're quite lucky! You must get a lot of Yakuman.
417:{character:player}If I knew I would be so lucky today, I would have bought a lottery ticket.
418:After speaking, I get ready to give Noah the cut flowers in my hands. However, she shakes her head and hands me one of her earphones instead.
419:{character:Kutsuji}(Voice message) Noah, you don't need to bring the flowers back with you later. Give them to {var:player_nickname} as a reward. Don't all children like to receive flowers as reward?
420:That's too much. Don't treat me like a child.
421:I resist the urge to reply with a retort and hold the expensive cut flowers tightly instead. Noah pats my shoulder, then she turns and gets into a red car next to her.
422:I rub my belly. What's done is done, so I decide to find a place to eat first. At least the task is going well so far. I'll worry about tomorrow's affairs tomorrow.
423:{character:player}What do you mean by "I guess it's okay"?
424:Noah looks at me, then she tilts her head and thinks for a while. After that, she lowers her head and sends me two text messages.
425:{character:Noah}(Text message) The boss knows what price each cut flower was sold for in the past few years. If you paid way too much for the cut flower, given his personality, he would definitely find a way to make you compensate him for the extra amount.
426:{character:Noah}(Text message) The current price should be within the range he can accept as long as you can complete the task.
427:{character:player}(Text message) He's an evil businessman, right?
428:{character:Noah}(Text message) I've taken a screenshot of your message and sent it to him.
429:{character:player}You actually said something out loud!
430:Noah takes a quick look at me before hastily covering her mouth with her hand. It looks like she realized she had spoken out loud. She lowers her head and taps the screen of her mobile phone, acting like she is refusing to communicate with me.
431:{character:Noah}(Text message) I just can't be bothered talking to people I'm not familiar with. It's not a big deal.
432:{character:player}(Text message) But we're acquaintances now. It's time to start communicating verbally.
433:{character:Noah}(Text message) I refuse.
434:{character:Noah}(Text message) Everyone other than Strix members is a stranger to me. I won't talk to you unless you have what it takes to become a member of Strix.
435:I let out a sigh. My ability to get on well with others doesn't seem to work with her. Why is that?
436:{character:player}What do you mean by "you're a goner"?
437:Noah glances at me. For some reason, the look she gives me seems to be full of pity.
